2026-09-06T22:43Z sim created
2026-09-06T22:43Z claude note Filed from the roar sweep after the 2026-09-06/07 session. Three failures in one night shared one shape, which is what makes this a class rather than three bugs. The sweep itself found no fourth instance -- all three resource:// paths the plugin currently names do exist in the shipped Zotero, verified against /opt/zotero7 -- so this ticket is the standing guard, not a repair.

2026-09-08T04:50Z claude note Guard landed as tests/test_sitter_host_apis.py: it reads the two omni.ja archives of an installed Zotero, derives the resource:// alias table from their own chrome.manifest files (only resource://gre/ is hard-coded, being Gecko's built-in root), and asserts every literal under plugins/ resolves to a real archive entry. Controls run both directions -- the Fluent.sys.mjs import written back into a plugin tree reddens and names its line, an undeclared host reddens rather than passing over, and the archive-reading half skips with a printed warning where no install is found. Ambient globals stay out of scope, named in the module docstring.
2026-09-08T05:24Z claude note Round 1 review fixes: the extraction is now anchored on an opening quote so a comment discussing a path is not read as a call site, and a fixture asserts that both ways rather than relying on today's comment wording; the alias chase runs to a fixpoint instead of one declaration-order-dependent pass; failure messages carry the install's version read off app/application.ini (10.0.1 here) so a verdict travels between machines; the historical-defect control compares against the live tree's own baseline, so a genuine breakage of the shipped plugin no longer reddens the control under a message naming the wrong defect; a fourth blind spot is documented -- a URL assembled by concatenation or interpolation passes the scan unseen, which a regex cannot fix. Quotes converted to the double-quote convention of the other 67 test files.
2026-09-08T05:59Z claude note Round 2 review fixes: whole-line comments are stripped before the scan, so a URL quoted inside the plugin's own narration no longer reads as a call site (the fixture now writes the historical string four ways, unquoted and quoted, // and block form; a URL quoted in a comment trailing a line of code remains a false red, never a false green, and is named as such); named_resource_urls reads with errors=replace like every other read in the file; ASSET_SUFFIXES records the classification of a format that cannot carry a literal, so the file-type guard fires on an icon by design rather than by accident. The last-wins resolution of a host declared twice is recorded as a limit rather than engineered around -- measured: Zotero 10.0.1 declares 21 hosts across the two manifests, none of them twice.
--- body ---
## Context

Three defects in one evening, all the same shape: the plugin asked the host
for something the host does not have, and every test agreed with it because
every test supplied the thing itself.

1. `ChromeUtils.importESModule('resource://gre/modules/Fluent.sys.mjs')`. No
   such module in Zotero 10.0.1. The import threw on every startup the feature
   ever had; the suites served Fluent through a MOCKED `importESModule`, so the
   call they exercised was one the real host has never answered. Forty green
   tests over a locale layer that was dead from its first commit.
2. `Zotero.File.getContentsFromURLAsync(rootURI + 'locale/…/x.ftl')`. In an
   installed add-on `rootURI` is `jar:file:///….xpi!/`, and that call cannot
   parse a `jar:` URI at all (`NS_ERROR_FAILURE [nsIURI.username]`). The tests
   served the `.ftl` files from disk by path, a delivery that has never once
   happened in production.
3. The same read for `manifest.json`, in `sitterStartupSelfCheck`. It has said
   `version: "unreadable (NS_ERROR_FAILURE)"` on every startup since ticket
   0688 added it -- in the field whose whole purpose is to name which build was
   running when the plugin disappears.

Each was found by the author running one line in his own Browser Console. None
was findable from this repository, because the repository contains no statement
of what the host provides that is checked against a host.

## What this ticket asks for

A guard that reads the plugin's host dependencies out of the source and asserts
each one exists in an installed Zotero. Not a mock: the real `omni.ja`.

Today that surface is small and entirely enumerable:

- `resource://gre/modules/Timer.sys.mjs`
- `resource://zotero/document-worker/structured-document-text.js`
- `resource://zotero/document-worker/metadata.json`

All three verified present on 2026-09-06 against `/opt/zotero7` -- the GRE jar
carries `modules/Timer.sys.mjs`, the app jar carries `resource/document-worker/…`
for the other two. The guard is what keeps that true, and what would have
reddened on `Fluent.sys.mjs` the day it was written.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — every `resource://` and `ChromeUtils`
  call site
- `tests/sdt_sitter_zotero_mock.mjs` — its `importESModule` now asserts an
  unexpected spec loudly rather than answering it, which is the same rule
  enforced from the other side
- `/opt/zotero7/omni.ja`, `/opt/zotero7/app/omni.ja` — the two archives that
  answer the question

## Actions

1. Grep the plugin source for `resource://…` literals and the host globals it
   reads off the scope (`FluentBundle` was one; `Services`, `ChromeUtils`,
   `IOUtils`, `PathUtils`, `Zotero` are the current set).
2. For each `resource://` path, resolve it into the corresponding archive entry
   (`resource://gre/x` -> `x` in the GRE jar; `resource://zotero/x` ->
   `resource/x` in the app jar) and assert the entry exists.
3. Mark it `slow` and SKIP -- explicitly, with a reason -- when no Zotero
   install is found. A guard that silently passes on a machine without the
   application is the failure this whole ticket is about, one level up.

## Test

The guard is the test. Its own control: point it at a path known to be absent
(`resource://gre/modules/Fluent.sys.mjs` is the honest fixture, since that is
the exact string that cost the evening) and confirm it reddens before trusting
it green on the real set.

## Verification

- [ ] Reddens on `Fluent.sys.mjs`, the real historical defect, not an invented one
- [ ] Greens on the three paths the plugin names today
- [ ] Skips loudly, with a reason, where no Zotero install exists
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- It reads the archives, never a mock. A mocked host asserting about the host
  is the defect, not the test.
- It does not attempt to verify ambient globals (`FluentBundle` and friends)
  the same way: those are compiled into the binary and named in no archive
  entry, which is exactly why the console was the only way to settle them.
  Naming that limit here is part of the guard; a guard whose blind spot is
  undocumented gets trusted past it.

## Exit criteria

Every `resource://` the sitter names is asserted against a real Zotero
installation, with a control run in both directions, and the guard says which
it could not check rather than passing over it.
